REGIONAL REGULATION OF SOUTH BANGKA REGENCY

NUMBER 9 OF 2018

CONCERNING

EMPOWERMENT AND PROTECTION OF WOMEN

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

REGENT OF SOUTH BANGKA,

Considering:

a. that women and men have equal rights to participate in various fields of life;
b. that women are national assets who play a very important role in the process of continuation and creation of quality generations, therefore they need to receive protection guarantees from acts of violence and discrimination and need to be empowered so that they can actualize their potential optimally;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, and letter b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Empowerment and Protection of Women;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 1 of 1974 concerning Marriage (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1974 Number 1, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3019);
3. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on The Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
4. Law Number 13 of 1998 concerning Welfare of the Elderly (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1998 Number 190, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3796);
5. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
6. Law Number 27 of 2000 concerning Establishment of Bangka Belitung Islands Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2000 Number 217, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4033);
7. Law Number 5 of 2003 concerning Establishment of South Bangka Regency, Central Bangka Regency, West Bangka Regency and East Belitung Regency in Bangka Belitung Islands Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 25, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4268);
8. Law Number 13 of 2003 concerning Manpower (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 39, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4279);
9. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
10. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
11. Law Number 11 of 2009 concerning Social Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 12, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4967);
12. Law Number 36 of 2009 concerning Health (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 144, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5063);
13. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
14. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587), as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
15. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 1 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for Improving the Quality of Life of Women;
16. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 2 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for Implementation of Protection of Women;

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF SOUTH BANGKA REGENCY

and

REGENT OF SOUTH BANGKA

DECIDES:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING EMPOWERMENT AND PROTECTION OF WOMEN.

Article 16

Implementation of protection of women is provided to:
a. female workers/laborers;
b. elderly women;
c. women with disabilities;
d. homeless women;
e. domestic workers;
f. women as family economic supporters;
g. women former inmates of correctional institutions;
h. women victims of disasters;
i. commercial sex workers; and
j. women victims of violence.

Article 4

Women's Empowerment and Protection aims to:
a. increase women's participation in the development process;
b. improve the quality of life of women;
c. provide guarantees to women in the fulfillment of rights as human beings; and.
d. provide a sense of security by increasing protection for women from various acts of violence.

Article 20

1. Implementation of protection for homeless women in order to provide protection and prevention against forms of violence caused by the absence of shelter or permanent residence.
2. Homeless women as referred to in paragraph (1) receive the right to protection for:
a. decent housing;
b. health and social security;
c. population administration registration;
d. opportunities to obtain skills to improve economic status; and
e. access to information and legal consultation services.

Article 19

1. Implementation of Protection for women with disabilities in order to provide protection and prevention against forms of discrimination including freedom from cruel, inhumane treatment, degrading human dignity and freedom from exploitation;
2. Women with disabilities as referred to in paragraph (1) receive the right to protection for:
a. guarantee of continuity and personal self-development;
b. participation in the fields of education, health, employment, politics, sports, arts and culture as well as the utilization of information and communication technology;
c. accessibility to information, legal aid, mobility, social services; and
d. early intervention efforts including treatment and rehabilitation to improve their function and capacity.

CHAPTER XII

GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

Article 32

1. The Regent or Regional Apparatus Organization in charge of women's empowerment shall conduct guidance and supervision over women's empowerment and protection;
2. Forms of guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) are as follows:
a. providing socialization to the community and business world regarding policies on women's empowerment and protection;
b. providing books, leaflets, brochures regarding women's empowerment and protection, and other related issues and disseminating them to the community;
c facilitation of capacity building for institutions organizing women's empowerment and protection;
d. facilitation of women's participation forums and women's organizations;
e. facilitation of rehabilitation and reintegration services for women disaster victims and victims of violence;
f. coordinating the participation of the business world and other institutions, including international assistance for women's empowerment and protection; and
g. providing awards to the community and business world both individually and institutionally who have made efforts for women's empowerment and protection.
3. Forms of supervision as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of monitoring and evaluation activities over women's empowerment and protection.

CHAPTER IV

WOMEN'S RIGHTS

Article 5

1. Every woman has the right to:
a. live and maintain life and improve her standard of living;
b. fulfill her basic needs to grow and develop properly, have a family and continue offspring;
c. develop her personality, to obtain education, and improve her quality of life to become a faithful, pious, responsible, noble, happy, and prosperous human being in accordance with human rights;
d. obtain justice, security, and freedom to express opinions without discrimination;
e. be involved in every stage of the development process;
f. be free from slavery or enslavement and threats;
g. obtain protection of her person, family, honor, dignity and property rights;
h. obtain welfare and a decent life;
i. participate in politics;
j. perform legal acts; and
k. freely choose a partner in her marriage.
2. Every wife during the marriage bond has the same rights and responsibilities as her husband over all matters relating to their married life, relations with their children, and ownership and management of joint property in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.
3. After the dissolution of marriage, a woman has rights and responsibilities with her former husband over all matters relating to children with due regard to the best interests of the child.

Article 17

1. Implementation of protection for female workers/laborers to provide protection from acts of economic exploitation and cruel, inhumane treatment and to seek proper appreciation for work.
2. Female workers/laborers as referred to in paragraph (1) receive protection for:
a. Recognition of rights, wages and decent working conditions;
b. Health insurance and employment social security;
c. Access to information and legal consultation services;
d. Opportunities to obtain knowledge and skills to improve status;

CHAPTER II

SCOPE

Article 2

The scope of this Regional Regulation includes:
a. Principles and objectives;
b. Women's rights;
c. Obligations and Responsibilities of Regional Government;
d. Women's Empowerment;
e. Women's Protection;
f. Strategy for Women's Empowerment and Protection;
g. Mechanism for Implementation of Women's Empowerment and Protection;
h. Community Participation;
i. Funding; and
j. Guidance and Supervision.

